{"title":"2025 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 10","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>An integer from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, inclusive, is randomly selected. What is the probability that the integer selected is a perfect square?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/a/59a985f3bac99c356c42d9c7bc2462f0b8af0b0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{3}{20} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{1}{9} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{1}{10} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{2}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"389\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can first realize that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/e/2aeebe42f3c014cdb186eb79d6c44801f846bd14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20 - 3 + 1 = 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"125\" height=\"15\" /> numbers.\n</p><p>Next, we can list the perfect squares between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. These would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/5/9a5b4928c8fe50ce3c2428da3bee3505e891b788.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> perfect squares. We could have also realized that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/b/8cb0e6e64d73bacef9c8869039bc21581f1884e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^2 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"50\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/8/128f11525fcf9981d3135935c9ea1784056b60ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4^2 = 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"17\" /> but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/3/3b31bdf57c202bda35b77d630124399deab12b96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^2 = 25$\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" />, so there were <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/8/d38d02091a90f0610762f83cdae80ac5cd08c222.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 - 2 + 1 = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"107\" height=\"15\" /> perfect squares.\n</p><p>The probability is then the number of perfect squares divided by the number of possible values.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/9/ad9ff3a7883ecf29ac73425b0756118574f40870.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{18}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Simplifying, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/b/ecb0d0701ed688758f4e8c84c8b37538d4ff1dfa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(C) } \\frac{1}{6}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"61\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"2025 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">2025 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2025 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2025 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2025 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 9\">Problem 9</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2025 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 11\">Problem 11</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/